<h1>POST /users 500 Error - Analysis, Root Cause & Solution Guide</h1>

<div class="doc-meta">
  <strong>Project:</strong> FamilyRoots / Family Sanctuary Application<br>
  <strong>Module:</strong> Backend User Management (NestJS + Prisma ORM + PostgreSQL)<br>
  <strong>Error Code:</strong> HTTP 500 (Internal Server Error) / Prisma P2002 / Postgres 23505<br>
  <strong>Date:</strong> September 20, 2026<br>
  <strong>Author:</strong> Antigravity Pair Programming Assistant
</div>

<h2>1. Executive Summary & Problem Statement</h2>
<p>
During user creation operations via the frontend dashboard or direct REST API requests to <code>POST http://localhost:8000/users</code>, the server unexpectedly crashed with an <strong>HTTP 500 (Internal Server Error)</strong>.
</p>
<div class="alert-box">
  <strong>Original Server Exception:</strong><br>
  <code>PrismaClientKnownRequestError: Unique constraint failed on the fields: (`email`)</code><br>
  <code>constraint: '23505', message: 'duplicate key value violates unique constraint "users_email_key"'</code>
</div>
<p>
This document details:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Why this error happened</strong> (the mechanical and architectural cause).</li>
  <li><strong>Where a user experiences this error</strong> in the application interface.</li>
  <li><strong>How it was solved</strong> on both the NestJS backend and Next.js frontend layers.</li>
  <li><strong>Best practices</strong> to ensure robust data integrity and user-friendly error handling.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. Detailed Root Cause Analysis: Why Did This Error Show?</h2>
<h3>2.1 Database Unique Constraint</h3>
<p>
In the PostgreSQL database schema (managed by Prisma in <code>prisma/schema/vault.prisma</code>), the <code>User</code> model defines the <code>email</code> field as unique:
</p>
<div class="code-block">model User {
  id            String    @id @default(cuid())
  email         String    @unique
  fullName      String
  password      String
  role          UserRole  @default(USER)
  ...
}</div>
<p>
When PostgreSQL executes an <code>INSERT INTO "users"</code> statement with an email that is identical to an existing record, the database enforces the unique index <code>users_email_key</code> and rejects the operation with error code <strong>23505</strong> (<code>unique_violation</code>).
</p>

<h3>2.2 Why Did It Return 500 Instead of 400 or 409?</h3>
<p>
In the previous implementation of <code>UsersService.create()</code> (located in <code>src/users/users.service.ts</code>), the method called Prisma directly without checking if the email already existed and without wrapping the database call in an exception handler:
</p>
<div class="code-block">// PREVIOUS UNPROTECTED CODE:
async create(createUserDto: any) {
  const { password, ...rest } = createUserDto;
  const plainPassword = password || 'SanctuaryPass123!';
  const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(plainPassword, 12);

  // Directly attempted INSERT without pre-check or try/catch:
  const user = await this.prisma.user.create({
    data: { ...rest, password: hashedPassword }
  });
  ...
}</div>
<p>
When Prisma threw <code>PrismaClientKnownRequestError (code P2002)</code>, NestJS treated it as an <strong>unhandled exception</strong>. By default, NestJS's built-in <code>ExceptionsHandler</code> catches unhandled exceptions and converts them into:
</p>
<div class="code-block">{
  "statusCode": 500,
  "message": "Internal server error"
}</div>
<p>
This caused the browser console to log:
<br>
<code>POST http://localhost:8000/users 500 (Internal Server Error)</code>
</p>

<h2>3. Where Does a User Encounter This Error?</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Interaction Location</th>
      <th>User Action</th>
      <th>Trigger Condition</th>
      <th>Previous Result</th>
      <th>Resolved Result</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Owner Dashboard</strong><br><code>/owner-dashboard/users</code></td>
      <td>Clicking <em>"+ Add User"</em> and submitting the user creation modal</td>
      <td>Entering an email address that belongs to an existing family member, owner, or previous user</td>
      <td><span class="badge-error">500 Server Error</span><br>"Failed to create user account."</td>
      <td><span class="badge-success">Immediate Warning</span><br>"A user account with this email already exists."</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Double Submission</strong></td>
      <td>Clicking the Submit button multiple times rapidly</td>
      <td>The first request creates the user; the second duplicate request fails</td>
      <td><span class="badge-error">500 Server Error</span></td>
      <td><span class="badge-success">409 Conflict</span><br>Gracefully handled</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Direct API Request</strong><br><code>POST /users</code></td>
      <td>External script or Postman sending creation payload</td>
      <td>Duplicate email payload</td>
      <td><span class="badge-error">500 Internal Error</span></td>
      <td><span class="badge-success">409 Conflict</span><br>Standard REST response</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>4. How Was It Solved? Complete Implementation</h2>

<div class="success-box">
  <strong>Dual-Layer Defense Strategy:</strong><br>
  1. <strong>Backend (NestJS):</strong> Added email normalization, pre-flight existence query, and defensive catch block converting <code>P2002</code> into standard <code>ConflictException</code> (HTTP 409).<br>
  2. <strong>Frontend (Next.js):</strong> Added client-side real-time duplicate detection against loaded users list to alert users before network requests.
</div>

<h3>4.1 Backend Solution (UsersService)</h3>
<p>File: <code>src/users/users.service.ts</code></p>
<div class="code-block">import {
  Injectable,
  NotFoundException,
  ConflictException,
  BadRequestException,
} from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class UsersService {
  ...

  async create(createUserDto: any) {
    const { password, email, ...rest } = createUserDto;
    const normalizedEmail = email ? email.trim().toLowerCase() : '';

    if (!normalizedEmail) {
      throw new BadRequestException('Email address is required.');
    }

    // Step 1: Check if user already exists
    const existingUser = await this.prisma.user.findUnique({
      where: { email: normalizedEmail },
    });

    if (existingUser) {
      throw new ConflictException(
        `A user account with email "${normalizedEmail}" already exists.`,
      );
    }

    const plainPassword = password || 'SanctuaryPass123!';
    const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(plainPassword, 12);

    // Step 2: Safe insertion with defensive error boundary
    try {
      const user = await this.prisma.user.create({
        data: {
          ...rest,
          email: normalizedEmail,
          password: hashedPassword,
        },
        select: {
          id: true,
          email: true,
          fullName: true,
          role: true,
          status: true,
        },
      });

      if (user.email) {
        this.mailService
          .sendMemberWelcomeEmail(user.email, user.fullName, user.email, plainPassword)
          .catch((err) => console.error('Failed to send user welcome email:', err));
      }

      return user;
    } catch (error: any) {
      // Catch concurrent race condition duplicate key errors
      if (
        error?.code === 'P2002' ||
        error?.message?.includes('Unique constraint failed') ||
        error?.message?.includes('duplicate key')
      ) {
        throw new ConflictException(
          `A user account with email "${normalizedEmail}" already exists.`,
        );
      }
      throw error;
    }
  }

  // Also applied to update():
  async update(id: string, updateUserDto: any) {
    const user = await this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
    if (!user) throw new NotFoundException('User not found');

    const data: any = { ...updateUserDto };
    if (data.email) {
      data.email = data.email.trim().toLowerCase();
      const existingWithEmail = await this.prisma.user.findUnique({
        where: { email: data.email },
      });
      if (existingWithEmail && existingWithEmail.id !== id) {
        throw new ConflictException(
          `A user account with email "${data.email}" already exists.`,
        );
      }
    }
    ...
  }
}</div>

<h3>4.2 Frontend Solution (User Accounts Page)</h3>
<p>File: <code>src/app/owner-dashboard/users/page.tsx</code></p>
<div class="code-block">const handleCreateUser = async (e: React.FormEvent) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  setFormError("");
  setFormSuccess("");

  if (!fullName.trim() || !email.trim()) {
    setFormError("Full name and email are required.");
    return;
  }

  const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  if (!emailRegex.test(email.trim())) {
    setFormError("Please enter a valid email address (e.g. user@example.com).");
    return;
  }

  // Instant Client-Side Duplicate Verification:
  const existingUser = usersList.find(
    (u) => u.email.toLowerCase() === email.trim().toLowerCase()
  );
  if (existingUser) {
    setFormError(
      `A user account with email "${email.trim()}" already exists (Role: ${existingUser.role}). You can update their role directly from the table below.`
    );
    return;
  }

  try {
    await createUser({
      fullName: fullName.trim(),
      email: email.trim(),
      password: password.trim() || "SanctuaryPass123!",
      role,
    }).unwrap();

    refetch();
    setFormSuccess(`User account created successfully as ${role}!`);
    ...
  } catch (err: any) {
    // Shows the exact server 409 message cleanly
    setFormError(
      err?.data?.message || err?.message || "Failed to create user account."
    );
  }
};</div>

<h2>5. Summary of Verification & Status</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Build Check:</strong> Backend compiles with 0 errors via <code>npm run build</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Type Check:</strong> Frontend compiles with 0 errors via <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>HTTP Status Code:</strong> Changed from unexpected <code>500 Internal Server Error</code> to standard <code>409 Conflict</code> with descriptive human-readable message.</li>
  <li><strong>User Experience:</strong> The user is never left with an opaque error screen or confusion about why an email cannot be registered twice.</li>
</ul>

